WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.439 --> 00:00:10.340
In 1838 the German educator Friedrich Fröbel
laid the foundations of modern education when

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:13.369
he opened the "Play and Activity Institute".

00:00:13.369 --> 00:00:19.440
Fröbel soon called his institute a Kindergarten
, reflecting his belief that young children

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:25.190
should be nurtured and nourished "like plants
in a garden”.

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:31.520
Fröbel, who studied under the Swiss educator
Johann Pestalozzi, established the idea that

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:36.890
games and playing are typical and essential
forms of life.

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:43.719
Activities in the kindergarten included singing,
dancing, gardening, and self-directed play.

00:00:43.719 --> 00:00:49.640
Quality time spent like this was a considerable
improvement to the life of many children,

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:54.390
given that the alternative was often to help
parents with work.

00:00:54.390 --> 00:01:02.039
He also introduced the concept of "Frei-Arbeit",
which can be translated into "free work".

00:01:02.039 --> 00:01:07.120
During set periods of time, children were
allowed to work on things by themselves.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:13.970
Where many adults saw pointless play, Fröbel
saw important learning taking place . While

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:18.970
practicing their concentration skills and
resilience,the children also learned about

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:24.000
engineering, logic and physics.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.490
To help facilitate this process, he developed
a set of educational toys known as Fröbel

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:37.040
Gifts . The set contained 20 objects, such
as balls, blocks, and sticks.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:42.000
Fröbel carefully designed the toys to help
the children in his kindergarten recognize

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.600
and appreciate common patterns and forms found
in nature.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:54.990
His innovative ideas soon found appeal and
many young educators came to learn from Fröbel

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:59.200
and to see the immense potential displayed
by children at his institute.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:05.120
Later, many of Fröbel’s students opened
their own Kindergarten and Germany experienced

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:09.670
a rapid growth in the numbers of early childhood
centers.

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:13.780
Then something bad happened.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:20.230
After suppressing the German revolutions of
1848–49, the Prussian government started

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:27.010
a crackdown on new democratic ideas and women
were forbidden from being politically active.

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:33.150
The fact that some were operating a kindergarten
all by themselves was seen as problematic.

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:39.690
And so it didn’t take long for the government
to label Fröbel’s Kindergarten ideas as

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:46.660
dangerous to both the State and church; soon
all schools that followed Fröbel’s principles

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.630
were banned.

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:55.730
For Fröbel, who saw his life’s work destroyed
and the future of all the children disrupted,

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:57.630
this was a terrible blow.

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:01.040
He died in dismay just a year later.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:07.210
But the ban caused a diaspora of kindergarteners
who could no longer work in Germany, spreading

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:10.640
Fröbel’s ideas all over the world.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:16.250
One of Fröbel's students founded the first
kindergarten in the United States in 1856.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:21.610
But the story was far from being over.

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:28.250
Exactly 20 years later, a young woman named
Anna Lloyd Jones stumbled upon a set of Fröbel’s

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:33.099
Gifts at a visit to the first World's Fair
in the United States.

00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:39.020
Anna, a teacher by training, was so excited
by the wooden toys that she bought a set for

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:42.959
her nine-year-old son.

00:03:42.959 --> 00:03:48.540
Little Frank loved the toys his mother brought
home and began building all kinds of geometrical

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:55.400
structures — first with the wooden toys,
later in miniatures and with other materials.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:04:00.099
Without formal training, Frank Lloyd Wright
became one of the world’s most renowned

00:04:00.099 --> 00:04:06.050
architects, responsible for some of the most
iconic buildings in modern architecture, many

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:11.540
of which resemble Fröbel's toy blocks.

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:18.100
Germany lifted its ban on kindergartens in
1860, realizing that it was a terrible mistake.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:22.750
And while Fröbel wasn’t around anymore
to witness the rebirth of his ideas in his

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:28.660
homeland, they continued to spread around
the world and became an inspiration for Maria

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:36.060
Montessori, Rudolf Steiner, and many others
who set out to innovate formal education.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:44.010
Fröbel once said that “Play is the highest
expression of human development in childhood,

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:49.450
for it alone is the free expression of what
is in a child's soul.”

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:54.430
Frank Lloyd Wright described the influence
of such play in his approach to design as

00:04:54.430 --> 00:05:00.840
follows: "For several years, I sat at the
little kindergarten table-top… and played…

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:06.080
with the cube, the sphere and the triangle—these
smooth wooden maple blocks…

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:11.310
All are in my fingers to this day.”

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:15.510
How was your personal experience with play
and learning when you were young?

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:17.570
Did you go to kindergarten?

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:22.240
And what are your thoughts on the play as
a way of growth and development?

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:24.090
Share your thoughts!

